But note: The problem says "adjacent swap" and we computed minimal adjacent swaps to achieve the grouped order with preserved relative order within groups. This is known to be optimal. However, is there any possibility that allowing parts to be empty and reordering within parts could lead to fewer swaps than the grouped order? Suppose we have L and M elements interleaved. The goal is to have all L before all M. Any adjacent swap that swaps an L past an M reduces the number of cross inversions. The minimal number of swaps to achieve that all L are before all M is equal to the number of pairs (L after M) in the original sequence, if we keep L order and M order. That's exactly the inversion count between L and M when considering L as "should come first". Similarly adding R after M adds inversions between M and R as well as L and R? But L and R must be in order L then M then R. So any pair where an R appears before an L or an M also creates an inversion relative to final order. Our target permutation accounts for all three groups. So yes.

